Lab Exercise 2 and 3: Overview of Management Information Systems (MIS)
A. Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:
1.	Describe the structure and components of a Management Information System.
2.	Distinguish MIS from traditional data processing systems.
3.	Analyze how MIS supports managerial decision-making.
4.	Explain the role of MIS in Information Resources Management (IRM).
5.	Apply MIS principles through practical tasks and case analysis.

B. Lab Activities
1. Structure of a Management Information System
Task 1: Draw the Structure of MIS
Create a diagram showing the typical structure of an MIS, including:
•	Input subsystem
•	Processing subsystem
•	Output/reporting subsystem
•	Database subsystem
•	Feedback & control mechanism
•	Users (operational, tactical, strategic)
Instructions:
Draw using lucid chart or word.
Task 2: MIS Structure Explanation
Write a short paragraph explaining how information flows from input → process → output within MIS.


2. MIS vs Data Processing
Task 3: Comparison Table
Prepare a table comparing MIS and Data Processing Systems based on:
•	Purpose
•	Input
•	Output
•	Users
•	Time orientation (past vs present/future)
•	Processing type
•	Complexity
•	Decision support capability
Example :
Feature	MIS	Data Processing
Purpose	Support decisions	Record transactions

3. Decision Making in MIS
Task 4: MIS Decision Scenario Using Spreadsheet
Use MS Excel / Google Sheets to simulate simple MIS decision support.
1.	Create a dataset with the following fields:
        o	Month
        o	Sales
        o	Cost
        o	Profit
2.	Enter data for 12 months.
3.	Generate the following MIS reports and decision tools:
    o	Total annual sales
    o	Monthly average profit
    o	Identify best month and worst month using formulas
    o	Use Goal Seek / What-If analysis to:
	Determine required sales to achieve a target annual profit
4.	Write a short explanation of how this simulated MIS report helps managerial decisions.


















Solution : TASK1
 

The Management Information System (MIS) is organized into several interconnected subsystems:
1.	Input Subsystem
o	Collects raw data from internal sources (transactions, operations) and external sources (market, suppliers).
o	Includes data entry, scanning, sensors, forms, etc.
2.	Processing Subsystem
o	Converts raw data into meaningful information through calculations, sorting, classification, analyzing, modeling, and summarizing.
3.	Database Subsystem
o	Central storage area managed by a DBMS.
o	Stores, organizes, secures, and retrieves data used by MIS reports.
4.	Output/Reporting Subsystem
o	Produces organized reports such as summary reports, exception reports, dashboards, charts, and decision-support information.
5.	Feedback & Control Mechanism
o	Evaluates system performance.
o	Compares actual output with expected output.
o	Sends corrective signals back to the input or processing subsystem.
6.	Users (Operational, Tactical, Strategic)
o	Operational-level users: daily routine tasks.
o	Tactical/managerial users: medium-term planning and control.
o	Strategic-level managers: long-term planning and policy decisions.
